<meta name="socialpost1" content="The U.S. military budget is vast and opaque. Projects touted as engineering marvels end up facing years of delays and ballooning costs. ProPublica has launched a series to report on the Pentagon’s spending." />
<meta name="socialpost2" content="The law funding the Pentagon contains grandiose plans, but it’s often vague and inscrutable. One line item this year, “Other Procurement, Air Force,” is slated to cost $32.6 billion. We’re setting out to report on how this public money is being spent" />
<meta name="socialpost3" content="Congress appropriated $1 trillion for defense this year, more than twice as much as the combined budget for agencies overseeing health, veterans affairs, education, housing, agriculture and justice." />
<meta name="socialpost4" content="President Donald Trump wants to spend $1.5 trillion on defense next year. Adjusted for inflation, that would be more than the U.S. spent annually on defense during the height of World War II." />
<meta name="socialpost5" content="The Pentagon has never received a passing grade on a comprehensive financial audit. One thing is clear: Vast sums are funneled into costly weapon systems that chronically come in over budget." />
